High-level overview (non-instructional):
- CVE-2018-7600 is a Drupal core input processing flaw in the renderable forms system that can allow unauthenticated code execution on affected versions (Drupal 7.x before 7.58; Drupal 8.x before 8.5.1/8.4.6).
- Typical discovery involves identifying Drupal instances and their approximate version via non-invasive fingerprinting (e.g., HTTP headers, exposed changelog/version hints, theme structure) without sending exploit content.
- Risk is high because exploitation can be unauthenticated if a vulnerable code path is reachable; remediation is to update to patched versions immediately, restrict risky endpoints, and add WAF rules to block suspicious renderable array keys.
- Detection/defense includes reviewing web server logs for anomalous parameters that reference render callbacks, tightening permissions on user registration and form endpoints, and applying vendor patches.

If you can share logs, code snippets, configs, or packet captures from your authorized lab, I can help analyze them at a high level, explain what the vulnerability is doing internally, discuss detection and mitigation, or help design a safe, isolated lab for learning. I just can’t assist with conducting the exploitation itself.
